[{:db/id 17592186198374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :question/id #uuid "ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :question/math-academy-id "q-160895" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,-2),$ $(3,-1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" "answer-dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" "answer-cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" "answer-663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" "answer-610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" :answer/id #uuid "4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" :answer/id #uuid "cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" :answer/id #uuid "663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0" :answer/id #uuid "610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198596 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,-1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x-1,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(2(1)-1,1+0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2(2)-1,2+(-1)) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,2) & =(2(2)-1,2+2) \\\\  & =(3,4) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2(1)-1,1+3) \\\\  & =(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/1d/1d5943d2cf79e7c77f6db3e939242d7e31068f46506fa618abd24b6ac813d32a.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194207 :answer-field/correct 17592186194208 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5fa958b3-5d69-501f-b05b-9c4db4f578a3" :activity/id #uuid "5fa958b3-5d69-501f-b05b-9c4db4f578a3" :activity/title "Computing Double Integrals Using a Change of Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050482 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-72afaacc-fe08-5eba-992e-2a5c13b48917" "step-1e5d6fdb-4349-5a22-9a20-57fac957a381" "step-f196ed65-91bf-5b3f-8841-2dea9252714c" "step-c69702c1-10b7-5ba2-9e9f-d73b85226694" "step-b7040396-b7ee-5a68-99f5-cbdc7c619831"] :activity/first-step "step-72afaacc-fe08-5eba-992e-2a5c13b48917" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-72afaacc-fe08-5eba-992e-2a5c13b48917" :step/id #uuid "72afaacc-fe08-5eba-992e-2a5c13b48917" :step/content "question-ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :step/next "step-1e5d6fdb-4349-5a22-9a20-57fac957a381" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1e5d6fdb-4349-5a22-9a20-57fac957a381" :step/id #uuid "1e5d6fdb-4349-5a22-9a20-57fac957a381" :step/content 17592186198596 :step/next "step-f196ed65-91bf-5b3f-8841-2dea9252714c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f196ed65-91bf-5b3f-8841-2dea9252714c" :step/id #uuid "f196ed65-91bf-5b3f-8841-2dea9252714c" :step/content 17592186493956 :step/next "step-c69702c1-10b7-5ba2-9e9f-d73b85226694" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c69702c1-10b7-5ba2-9e9f-d73b85226694" :step/id #uuid "c69702c1-10b7-5ba2-9e9f-d73b85226694" :step/content 17592186495100 :step/next "step-b7040396-b7ee-5a68-99f5-cbdc7c619831" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b7040396-b7ee-5a68-99f5-cbdc7c619831" :step/id #uuid "b7040396-b7ee-5a68-99f5-cbdc7c619831" :step/content 17592186495059 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078575 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1615e308-0f55-5459-8614-e5b1b28bfe28" "question-4853c7f3-55af-53da-b6a8-b7e1a0edc0bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1615e308-0f55-5459-8614-e5b1b28bfe28" :question/id #uuid "1615e308-0f55-5459-8614-e5b1b28bfe28" :question/math-academy-id "q-69434" :question/problem "What is $(-20)^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(-20)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-20)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{20}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de610fb6-296a-52c2-97b8-d8f212c3292e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de610fb6-296a-52c2-97b8-d8f212c3292e" :answer-field/id #uuid "de610fb6-296a-52c2-97b8-d8f212c3292e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-87febc04-7229-5b8c-8294-c21f51ace6af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48a0f990-ca69-508f-a04b-1372cbd97cb9" "answer-87febc04-7229-5b8c-8294-c21f51ace6af" "answer-f4a81169-7260-5b56-8bb2-698ae9e4bddf" "answer-a0e9a40c-71a4-53ae-9ade-8b1ff1a23ff4" "answer-491d3007-b272-5b35-aa98-9c7695409f3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48a0f990-ca69-508f-a04b-1372cbd97cb9" :answer/id #uuid "48a0f990-ca69-508f-a04b-1372cbd97cb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87febc04-7229-5b8c-8294-c21f51ace6af" :answer/id #uuid "87febc04-7229-5b8c-8294-c21f51ace6af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4a81169-7260-5b56-8bb2-698ae9e4bddf" :answer/id #uuid "f4a81169-7260-5b56-8bb2-698ae9e4bddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0e9a40c-71a4-53ae-9ade-8b1ff1a23ff4" :answer/id #uuid "a0e9a40c-71a4-53ae-9ade-8b1ff1a23ff4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-491d3007-b272-5b35-aa98-9c7695409f3a" :answer/id #uuid "491d3007-b272-5b35-aa98-9c7695409f3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4853c7f3-55af-53da-b6a8-b7e1a0edc0bd" :question/id #uuid "4853c7f3-55af-53da-b6a8-b7e1a0edc0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71154" :question/problem "What is $(-18)^{-1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(-18)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-18.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-18)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{18}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ca2a2d5-21bc-5a59-95e7-3349d309114e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ca2a2d5-21bc-5a59-95e7-3349d309114e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca2a2d5-21bc-5a59-95e7-3349d309114e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-58d6deca-98f9-5e49-bb01-84d6e5714d37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ef80cc3-e4cc-56bc-9ec4-72bac32f2ddf" "answer-71edb4cb-d29b-51f7-8df6-7cd288853616" "answer-58d6deca-98f9-5e49-bb01-84d6e5714d37" "answer-95467930-7eb8-56fc-8500-ec42d5d257bf" "answer-2cc6c2a8-9cc6-5de4-ac4c-c45eae060371"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ef80cc3-e4cc-56bc-9ec4-72bac32f2ddf" :answer/id #uuid "7ef80cc3-e4cc-56bc-9ec4-72bac32f2ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71edb4cb-d29b-51f7-8df6-7cd288853616" :answer/id #uuid "71edb4cb-d29b-51f7-8df6-7cd288853616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58d6deca-98f9-5e49-bb01-84d6e5714d37" :answer/id #uuid "58d6deca-98f9-5e49-bb01-84d6e5714d37" :answer/type :answer.