answer-field/choices 17592186187927] [:db/retract 17592186187926 :answer-field/choices 17592186187928] [:db/retract 17592186187926 :answer-field/choices 17592186187929] [:db/retract 17592186187926 :answer-field/choices 17592186187930] [:db/retract 17592186187926 :answer-field/choices 17592186187931] [:db/retract 17592186187548 :answer-field/choices 17592186187552] [:db/retract 17592186187548 :answer-field/choices 17592186187553] [:db/retract 17592186187548 :answer-field/choices 17592186187549] [:db/retract 17592186187548 :answer-field/choices 17592186187550] [:db/retract 17592186187548 :answer-field/choices 17592186187551] {:db/id 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" "question-1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" "question-70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" "question-94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" "question-9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" "question-bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" "question-842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" "question-a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" :question/id #uuid "3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-119185" :question/problem "An oil tank has a leak. The rate of change of the volume $V$ (in liters) of oil in the tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) passed since the tank started leaking. However, the tank is also refilled with $4$ liters of oil every minute. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of oil in the tank at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the oil volume decreases in proportion to time $t$ and increases by $4$ liters per minute. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-63b0ba67-775a-55e5-baad-f129063461aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-63b0ba67-775a-55e5-baad-f129063461aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "63b0ba67-775a-55e5-baad-f129063461aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a62277eb-8471-5a33-82c1-9e84c22b8e8f" "answer-6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" "answer-be1dca02-392a-5ab7-8831-c08cee6fac63" "answer-705637e0-e025-5947-a35a-621d180f650c" "answer-b8e4717f-809c-579d-82ee-5d76825ca02e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a62277eb-8471-5a33-82c1-9e84c22b8e8f" :answer/id #uuid "a62277eb-8471-5a33-82c1-9e84c22b8e8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4kt,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" :answer/id #uuid "6d780f55-423d-520a-ae84-39c36f6ee582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be1dca02-392a-5ab7-8831-c08cee6fac63" :answer/id #uuid "be1dca02-392a-5ab7-8831-c08cee6fac63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt-4,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-705637e0-e025-5947-a35a-621d180f650c" :answer/id #uuid "705637e0-e025-5947-a35a-621d180f650c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{t}+4,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8e4717f-809c-579d-82ee-5d76825ca02e" :answer/id #uuid "b8e4717f-809c-579d-82ee-5d76825ca02e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kt+4,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee" :question/id #uuid "1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-79310" :question/problem "A cyclist has a small puncture in one of her tires. The volume of air $V,$ in centimeters cubed, in the tire decreases at a rate that's proportional to the volume at that moment. Every day, the cyclist pumps $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ back into the tire.\nWhat is the differential equation describing the rate of change of the volume of air in the tire  at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in days?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the volume of air per day decreases in proportion to $V$ and increases by $12.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00c35029-688f-585b-b372-8febae786a22"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00c35029-688f-585b-b372-8febae786a22" :answer-field/id #uuid "00c35029-688f-585b-b372-8febae786a22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cd3f9812-08b3-55fc-a348-c9861184305c" "answer-0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" "answer-754197e2-db12-5135-a97c-80f3df146721" "answer-105b83e4-246b-55e2-b4ab-738341a8abea" "answer-7bdf12f4-4e3f-5902-b339-3b9f7049068e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3f9812-08b3-55fc-a348-c9861184305c" :answer/id #uuid "cd3f9812-08b3-55fc-a348-c9861184305c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-V-10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" :answer/id #uuid "0fc830cc-ece8-5496-97a7-70e65565df26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754197e2-db12-5135-a97c-80f3df146721" :answer/id #uuid "754197e2-db12-5135-a97c-80f3df146721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV-10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-105b83e4-246b-55e2-b4ab-738341a8abea" :answer/id #uuid "105b83e4-246b-55e2-b4ab-738341a8abea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=V-10k,\\,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bdf12f4-4e3f-5902-b339-3b9f7049068e" :answer/id #uuid "7bdf12f4-4e3f-5902-b339-3b9f7049068e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV+10,\\,\\,k>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" :question/id #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143110" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9cf447a-a943-5b06-b1a6-df34fd52eceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" "answer-d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" "answer-566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" "answer-ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" "answer-ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" :answer/id #uuid "e8c89197-063d-5efb-987f-b5a6be72e4d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" :answer/id #uuid "d478dfbd-05e0-5dc2-bc83-3caf65a81571" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=kxy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" :answer/id #uuid "566b270e-601c-556c-909e-228f098bd523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" :answer/id #uuid "ccb54fc0-7b29-5b8d-9591-693a024ce49a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/id #uuid "ffd59d23-9e44-5a2b-9d57-caf1ff79fe38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" :question/id #uuid "70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143109" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $y$-axis * is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot |x|,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k|x|.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20aeab36-0a5e-5c4e-bb58-d247747394e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20aeab36-0a5e-5c4e-bb58-d247747394e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "20aeab36-0a5e-5c4e-bb58-d247747394e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3bef70d9-620b-5bc2-9ce0-41b67d6e9c77" "answer-fef5b82d-787d-5e00-8130-1277c4a51a73" "answer-83cd57a1-e672-5e0d-b765-9410b2bf93a4" "answer-d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" "answer-f46f16a9-9135-5dc7-811d-9d40d9a99198"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bef70d9-620b-5bc2-9ce0-41b67d6e9c77" :answer/id #uuid "3bef70d9-620b-5bc2-9ce0-41b67d6e9c77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef5b82d-787d-5e00-8130-1277c4a51a73" :answer/id #uuid "fef5b82d-787d-5e00-8130-1277c4a51a73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83cd57a1-e672-5e0d-b765-9410b2bf93a4" :answer/id #uuid "83cd57a1-e672-5e0d-b765-9410b2bf93a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" :answer/id #uuid "d0606410-77b4-5ccd-9d71-85600bd1e077" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46f16a9-9135-5dc7-811d-9d40d9a99198" :answer/id #uuid "f46f16a9-9135-5dc7-811d-9d40d9a99198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k|y|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" :question/id #uuid "94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143107" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **squared distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *squared distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03510cef-4190-5c10-95bf-9938ff3a5ecb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03510cef-4190-5c10-95bf-9938ff3a5ecb" :answer-field/id #uuid "03510cef-4190-5c10-95bf-9938ff3a5ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" "answer-ca9a3d00-b902-540f-adc1-1a154ca1cb1f" "answer-a89759bb-3bac-504e-b772-0676db77d102" "answer-769bd46c-ac71-5c9d-86aa-64d30f596ff8" "answer-e300ec87-21ad-50b7-8b42-3adbf0764a32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" :answer/id #uuid "83bbabc7-4044-5aff-aa2b-c2aefecaf00b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9a3d00-b902-540f-adc1-1a154ca1cb1f" :answer/id #uuid "ca9a3d00-b902-540f-adc1-1a154ca1cb1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a89759bb-3bac-504e-b772-0676db77d102" :answer/id #uuid "a89759bb-3bac-504e-b772-0676db77d102" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-769bd46c-ac71-5c9d-86aa-64d30f596ff8" :answer/id #uuid "769bd46c-ac71-5c9d-86aa-64d30f596ff8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e300ec87-21ad-50b7-8b42-3adbf0764a32" :answer/id #uuid "e300ec87-21ad-50b7-8b42-3adbf0764a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" :question/id #uuid "9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" :question/math-academy-id "q-143142" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. The derivative at the point $(-3,6)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis* is given by $|y|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |y|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|y|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(-3,6).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=k|6|\\,⟹\\,k=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-917748b4-b224-5d7a-96ef-e578265796e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-917748b4-b224-5d7a-96ef-e578265796e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "917748b4-b224-5d7a-96ef-e578265796e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" "answer-adef745d-e631-557e-874a-c44d15d825e8" "answer-cd022d2f-f204-5579-bb94-25cfa4ed54b7" "answer-c1525df8-367a-5d9b-b7b9-3446e2adefb1" "answer-b8426d7e-d065-50db-bf82-777624091a2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" :answer/id #uuid "9494b15f-f120-5e41-bde4-5fecd3535616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adef745d-e631-557e-874a-c44d15d825e8" :answer/id #uuid "adef745d-e631-557e-874a-c44d15d825e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=4|x|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd022d2f-f204-5579-bb94-25cfa4ed54b7" :answer/id #uuid "cd022d2f-f204-5579-bb94-25cfa4ed54b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|x|}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1525df8-367a-5d9b-b7b9-3446e2adefb1" :answer/id #uuid "c1525df8-367a-5d9b-b7b9-3446e2adefb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|y|}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8426d7e-d065-50db-bf82-777624091a2e" :answer/id #uuid "b8426d7e-d065-50db-bf82-777624091a2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|y|}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" :question/id #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-144820" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fe16f66-cbec-5dab-913b-319990c8cc5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" "answer-a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" "answer-89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" "answer-e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" "answer-c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" :answer/id #uuid "59b992c6-a6c5-5a2a-bea8-00115a148a8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" :answer/id #uuid "a3ff4381-b2a5-5d9c-a6aa-03d02c2f7df0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/id #uuid "89ee39bb-f1f9-5ca9-937e-5d500320a292" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" :answer/id #uuid "e9d8375a-8649-5db7-9cc9-00f70e9c364c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{6y}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b" :answer/id #uuid "c4209c74-841c-5648-8653-0db964c34b5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188542 :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185532 :answer-field/correct "answer-f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" "answer-f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" "answer-7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" "answer-2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" "answer-62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" :answer/id #uuid "0ce17c65-7d67-54af-bb8e-a4b548957fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/id #uuid "f60054c5-79dc-51b2-9d83-1ea1d762a52d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" :answer/id #uuid "7e1c7097-61f8-5853-b475-d4c6e09f84d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" :answer/id #uuid "2fc573f9-6cba-59f5-957c-6c9a34a77f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b" :answer/id #uuid "62f66835-664a-578e-9ff4-452e7799954b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=r+K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" :question/id #uuid "8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-143134" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate proportional to the cube of its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **cube of its perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the cube of its perimeter*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k(4s)^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =k(4s)^{3} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =64k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64k{s}^{3}}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =32k{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=32k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e953d84f-9d70-56d1-a2e6-25501f223dc2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e953d84f-9d70-56d1-a2e6-25501f223dc2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e953d84f-9d70-56d1-a2e6-25501f223dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b11d0e7a-c89d-5592-b063-2bcfc835c8a9" "answer-53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" "answer-85fe4b15-915f-5ca8-a203-8af14de723fc" "answer-cca7a708-1831-5d8d-95ae-358f0a1f9723" "answer-0b33e11a-e522-5400-8bb3-afd63970b187"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b11d0e7a-c89d-5592-b063-2bcfc835c8a9" :answer/id #uuid "b11d0e7a-c89d-5592-b063-2bcfc835c8a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=Ks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" :answer/id #uuid "53318ba9-1b15-543d-9d80-95909854731c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85fe4b15-915f-5ca8-a203-8af14de723fc" :answer/id #uuid "85fe4b15-915f-5ca8-a203-8af14de723fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cca7a708-1831-5d8d-95ae-358f0a1f9723" :answer/id #uuid "cca7a708-1831-5d8d-95ae-358f0a1f9723" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b33e11a-e522-5400-8bb3-afd63970b187" :answer/id #uuid "0b33e11a-e522-5400-8bb3-afd63970b187" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332" :question/id #uuid "842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332" :question/math-academy-id "q-143130" :question/problem "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to **the square of its circumference**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to *the square of its circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}{r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5708293f-0ffb-5c47-a00d-e170d13fb197"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5708293f-0ffb-5c47-a00d-e170d13fb197" :answer-field/id #uuid "5708293f-0ffb-5c47-a00d-e170d13fb197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f157fdf-0dbd-517d-b358-049a5969dec3" "answer-23680e06-ba93-52a3-b975-55fcee12e521" "answer-e597013b-e59c-54da-9a35-6be52c89dceb" "answer-0769ab1a-87bc-5eff-8aeb-99c8fc7755ee" "answer-44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f157fdf-0dbd-517d-b358-049a5969dec3" :answer/id #uuid "8f157fdf-0dbd-517d-b358-049a5969dec3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23680e06-ba93-52a3-b975-55fcee12e521" :answer/id #uuid "23680e06-ba93-52a3-b975-55fcee12e521" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e597013b-e59c-54da-9a35-6be52c89dceb" :answer/id #uuid "e597013b-e59c-54da-9a35-6be52c89dceb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0769ab1a-87bc-5eff-8aeb-99c8fc7755ee" :answer/id #uuid "0769ab1a-87bc-5eff-8aeb-99c8fc7755ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc" :answer/id #uuid "44e2023a-8e7d-5888-a40a-68baea3726dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188975 :question/problem "The volume of a cube decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=6{s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6k{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6k{s}^{2}}{3{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186187926 :answer-field/correct "answer-e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" "answer-e7efebb7-1731-5b5b-bba6-ee401ac97130" "answer-5e91d50d-2e23-50af-b2c1-3fb740beeb3a" "answer-74861129-d701-503d-80d2-8fd2c0956239" "answer-0fce5104-1668-5712-b8bd-4600d8fe3f4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" :answer/id #uuid "e573b28e-6822-53dc-896b-d87246576b05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7efebb7-1731-5b5b-bba6-ee401ac97130" :answer/id #uuid "e7efebb7-1731-5b5b-bba6-ee401ac97130" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-Ks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e91d50d-2e23-50af-b2c1-3fb740beeb3a" :answer/id #uuid "5e91d50d-2e23-50af-b2c1-3fb740beeb3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K{s}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74861129-d701-503d-80d2-8fd2c0956239" :answer/id #uuid "74861129-d701-503d-80d2-8fd2c0956239" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fce5104-1668-5712-b8bd-4600d8fe3f4d" :answer/id #uuid "0fce5104-1668-5712-b8bd-4600d8fe3f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" :question/id #uuid "9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" :question/math-academy-id "q-38598" :question/problem "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc9d2d16-1277-5174-8bfa-2ed096aba409"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc9d2d16-1277-5174-8bfa-2ed096aba409" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d2d16-1277-5174-8bfa-2ed096aba409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" "answer-2a7d0bb7-352a-5bf9-a7f5-302351f7d4e8" "answer-37965923-72fe-55e3-94ae-308e2569746c" "answer-46633668-b0a3-53ef-86c3-cafb733f4e7c" "answer-b5b2d8d2-d36b-5317-a242-1e5495b2e405"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" :answer/id #uuid "20ad34be-911c-538b-9802-3b400981630c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a7d0bb7-352a-5bf9-a7f5-302351f7d4e8" :answer/id #uuid "2a7d0bb7-352a-5bf9-a7f5-302351f7d4e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37965923-72fe-55e3-94ae-308e2569746c" :answer/id #uuid "37965923-72fe-55e3-94ae-308e2569746c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46633668-b0a3-53ef-86c3-cafb733f4e7c" :answer/id #uuid "46633668-b0a3-53ef-86c3-cafb733f4e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{r}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5b2d8d2-d36b-5317-a242-1e5495b2e405" :answer/id #uuid "b5b2d8d2-d36b-5317-a242-1e5495b2e405" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188912 :question/problem "Tom's spherical basketball has a hole in it. Its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of the ball. Assume that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186187548 :answer-field/correct "answer-638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8793e775-b1b8-51bf-8191-d8fb79ae27e8" "answer-0bcce3e0-856f-50b2-a996-ed820481fba7" "answer-638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" "answer-021cf654-7170-5d38-a79a-42fe86f478ee" "answer-14c405ae-7793-5579-b31f-2de4fa47beb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8793e775-b1b8-51bf-8191-d8fb79ae27e8" :answer/id #uuid "8793e775-b1b8-51bf-8191-d8fb79ae27e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-3kr" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bcce3e0-856f-50b2-a996-ed820481fba7" :answer/id #uuid "0bcce3e0-856f-50b2-a996-ed820481fba7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" :answer/id #uuid "638194a1-7794-5512-a783-f3c9c287538f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-021cf654-7170-5d38-a79a-42fe86f478ee" :answer/id #uuid "021cf654-7170-5d38-a79a-42fe86f478ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-kr+2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c405ae-7793-5579-b31f-2de4fa47beb3" :answer/id #uuid "14c405ae-7793-5579-b31f-2de4fa47beb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" :question/id #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-66523" :question/problem "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367" :answer-field/id #uuid "e801f5f0-f458-5f7b-91b1-d27a8bacf367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" "answer-c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" "answer-dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" "answer-61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" "answer-f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/id #uuid "70e8b4fb-f4e5-5b00-9546-7908b751149c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" :answer/id #uuid "c817eb95-9eb3-52b3-8f8b-7dc081fb2ab4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr+t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" :answer/id #uuid "dfc3c7fc-fe9f-5c7b-8cbd-d47330b88535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" :answer/id #uuid "61117aac-0ddd-5b18-90b2-d250afca608b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a" :answer/id #uuid "f3ff4ab0-084f-5bf6-8cc1-99171d95158a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kt" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189375 :tutorial/content "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189439 :tutorial/content "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188455 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" "question-b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" :question/id #uuid "7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-44202" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,7)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{7-0}{4-0}=\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(0,0).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{7}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y) & =4\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4y & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7x \\\\ -7x+4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+4y & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x-4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $7x-4y=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf0b3dce-d893-5070-8809-d9f2b47f02b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf0b3dce-d893-5070-8809-d9f2b47f02b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0b3dce-d893-5070-8809-d9f2b47f02b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a1bd908-d894-5634-a386-eae6d2558b62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81c06851-5a55-515e-b014-4e1a61503690" "answer-4948554b-8702-53e2-9b75-d60c9757c1b2" "answer-fc01bcb9-ef79-5851-a139-46cdb6164d5e" "answer-9a1bd908-d894-5634-a386-eae6d2558b62" "answer-ace9b919-181f-5f85-aacf-3f2308c62bcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c06851-5a55-515e-b014-4e1a61503690" :answer/id #uuid "81c06851-5a55-515e-b014-4e1a61503690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y=3x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4948554b-8702-53e2-9b75-d60c9757c1b2" :answer/id #uuid "4948554b-8702-53e2-9b75-d60c9757c1b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-2y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc01bcb9-ef79-5851-a139-46cdb6164d5e" :answer/id #uuid "fc01bcb9-ef79-5851-a139-46cdb6164d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4y=7x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a1bd908-d894-5634-a386-eae6d2558b62" :answer/id #uuid "9a1bd908-d894-5634-a386-eae6d2558b62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7x-4y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ace9b919-181f-5f85-aacf-3f2308c62bcf" :answer/id #uuid "ace9b919-181f-5f85-aacf-3f2308c62bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-\\frac{4}{7}\\,y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6" :question/id #uuid "b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72425" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(1,-2)$ and $(3,6)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-(-2)}{3-1}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(1,-2).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4x-4 \\\\ y & =4x-6 \\\\ -4x+y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+y & =-6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ 4x-y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $4x-y=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ee1d5f3-79b0-529f-9d27-584c54dd513d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ee1d5f3-79b0-529f-9d27-584c54dd513d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee1d5f3-79b0-529f-9d27-584c54dd513d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-aa1d2cc4-2934-5c01-acf3-e4688de64dff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca9d0db1-1d42-546b-8c61-bc0bc89d225c" "answer-c0d0840e-b8f1-5833-899d-775ab6668a32" "answer-aa1d2cc4-2934-5c01-acf3-e4688de64dff" "answer-cf99202c-1f33-5c86-9aa3-ff042e4be5ae" "answer-c3d3f7b4-e921-553d-88ad-be60876e12b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9d0db1-1d42-546b-8c61-bc0bc89d225c" :answer/id #uuid "ca9d0db1-1d42-546b-8c61-bc0bc89d225c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4x+y=-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0d0840e-b8f1-5833-899d-775ab6668a32" :answer/id #uuid "c0d0840e-b8f1-5833-899d-775ab6668a32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-y=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa1d2cc4-2934-5c01-acf3-e4688de64dff" :answer/id #uuid "aa1d2cc4-2934-5c01-acf3-e4688de64dff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x-y=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf99202c-1f33-5c86-9aa3-ff042e4be5ae" :answer/id #uuid "cf99202c-1f33-5c86-9aa3-ff042e4be5ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d3f7b4-e921-553d-88ad-be60876e12b6" :answer/id #uuid "c3d3f7b4-e921-553d-88ad-be60876e12b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1" :question/id #uuid "654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109618" :question/problem "Write $3-4y=-3x$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-4y=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-4y=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ecaf1bd-48c2-52f6-ac2e-e2c732914f1d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ecaf1bd-48c2-52f6-ac2e-e2c732914f1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecaf1bd-48c2-52f6-ac2e-e2c732914f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ec6efbc0-8d44-5de2-87c8-4041efd1a2f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd48f16b-2a4a-5029-a026-63bc2965823a" "answer-ec6efbc0-8d44-5de2-87c8-4041efd1a2f6" "answer-3ff46960-a634-5a81-ae1f-2ca39d897a7e" "answer-79487712-6da4-5496-8225-aac3af672e2a" "answer-b8f9749e-7d8c-55a2-9037-1039c6ab8a8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd48f16b-2a4a-5029-a026-63bc2965823a" :answer/id #uuid "dd48f16b-2a4a-5029-a026-63bc2965823a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-4y+3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec6efbc0-8d44-5de2-87c8-4041efd1a2f6" :answer/id #uuid "ec6efbc0-8d44-5de2-87c8-4041efd1a2f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-4y=-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ff46960-a634-5a81-ae1f-2ca39d897a7e" :answer/id #uuid "3ff46960-a634-5a81-ae1f-2ca39d897a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x=4y-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79487712-6da4-5496-8225-aac3af672e2a" :answer/id #uuid "79487712-6da4-5496-8225-aac3af672e2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4y=3x+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f9749e-7d8c-55a2-9037-1039c6ab8a8e" :answer/id #uuid "b8f9749e-7d8c-55a2-9037-1039c6ab8a8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2y=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ea710b72-1ae9-594f-81f0-bb4e388075ac" :activity/id #uuid "ea710b72-1ae9-594f-81f0-bb4e388075ac" :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049840 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-b7421da9-e8bc-5823-9858-330f84d2ecf1" "step-dbbb55d2-6ac7-5cb1-a4a3-8f1b82a1b491" "step-20f07fd1-d467-5612-a99b-42e2c6facda8"] :activity/first-step "step-b7421da9-e8bc-5823-9858-330f84d2ecf1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b7421da9-e8bc-5823-9858-330f84d2ecf1" :step/id #uuid "b7421da9-e8bc-5823-9858-330f84d2ecf1" :step/content "question-7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" :step/next "step-dbbb55d2-6ac7-5cb1-a4a3-8f1b82a1b491" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dbbb55d2-6ac7-5cb1-a4a3-8f1b82a1b491" :step/id #uuid "dbbb55d2-6ac7-5cb1-a4a3-8f1b82a1b491" :step/content "question-b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6" :step/next "step-20f07fd1-d467-5612-a99b-42e2c6facda8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-20f07fd1-d467-5612-a99b-42e2c6facda8" :step/id #uuid "20f07fd1-d467-5612-a99b-42e2c6facda8" :step/content "question-654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186176232 :question/answer-fields 17592186173650] {:db/id 17592186175993 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25" :question/id #uuid "18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-109747" :question/problem "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{5}(\\lambda +3)^{2},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{2}=3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc7e5239-25ea-5909-b9ce-6ad812ed18c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc7e5239-25ea-5909-b9ce-6ad812ed18c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e5239-25ea-5909-b9ce-6ad812ed18c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67237cb474f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67237cb474f5" "answer-84bfa36c-7054-5b53-81f3-b9e1906d6186" "answer-1997f878-2762-56d9-ac49-b26b9ac9edc2" "answer-379bda0c-41bc-5297-b017-d518173b466b" "answer-c9c4aa88-43ec-5d07-ae83-df734fe4814c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67237cb474f5" :answer/id #uuid "58f930ba-330b-56ca-943a-67